---
title: dmesg
order: 3
description: dmesg, kernel ring buffer, filtering, follow
tags: [dmesg, kernel, ring-buffer, logs, debug]
---

dmesg reads the kernel ring buffer — the primary source for hardware, driver, and early boot messages.

@text
Basic Usage

@code bash
# Print all kernel messages
dmesg

# Print with human-readable timestamps
dmesg -T

# Print with timestamps and color
dmesg -T --color=always

# Print with ISO timestamps
dmesg --time-format=iso

# Follow new messages in real-time (like tail -f)
dmesg -w
dmesg -wT    # follow with human timestamps

# Show only new messages since last read
dmesg -c     # print and clear (requires root)

@text
Filtering by Level

Log levels from 0 (most critical) to 7 (debug).

@code bash
# Show only errors and worse
dmesg --level=err
dmesg -l err

# Show warnings and errors
dmesg -l warn,err

# Show only critical/emergency
dmesg -l emerg,alert,crit

# Log levels:
# emerg (0)  — system is unusable
# alert (1)  — immediate action required
# crit  (2)  — critical conditions
# err   (3)  — error conditions
# warn  (4)  — warning conditions
# notice(5)  — normal but significant
# info  (6)  — informational
# debug (7)  — debug messages

@text
Filtering by Facility

@code bash
# Show only kernel messages
dmesg -f kern

# Show only user-space messages
dmesg -f user

# Show only daemon messages
dmesg -f daemon

# Available facilities:
# kern, user, mail, daemon, auth, syslog, lpr, news

@text
Searching and Filtering

@code bash
# Search for specific hardware
dmesg | grep -i usb
dmesg | grep -i nvidia
dmesg | grep -i "error\|fail\|warn"

# Show messages from the last boot only (ring buffer is per-boot)
dmesg -T | head -50

# Find network interface messages
dmesg | grep -iE "eth|enp|wlan|wlp|net"

# Find disk/storage messages
dmesg | grep -iE "sd[a-z]|nvme|ata|scsi"

# Find filesystem messages
dmesg | grep -iE "ext4|btrfs|xfs|mount"

# Show the last N lines
dmesg | tail -20

# Show first N lines (early boot)
dmesg | head -30

@text
Clearing and Buffer Info

@code bash
# Clear the ring buffer (requires root)
sudo dmesg -C

# Print then clear
sudo dmesg -c

# Show buffer size
dmesg -s 1048576    # set read buffer size (bytes)

# Check kernel log buffer size (set at boot)
cat /proc/cmdline | grep -o "log_buf_len=[^ ]*"

@text
Practical Examples

@code bash
# Diagnose USB device detection
dmesg -wT | grep -i usb
# Then plug in the device

# Check for hardware errors after boot
dmesg -T -l err,crit,alert,emerg

# Find when a device was detected
dmesg -T | grep -i "new high-speed USB"

# Check memory info at boot
dmesg | grep -i "memory\|mem"

# Check CPU info
dmesg | grep -i "cpu\|microcode"

# Find out of memory (OOM) kills
dmesg -T | grep -i "out of memory\|oom"

# Monitor for thermal events
dmesg -wT | grep -i "thermal\|temperature"
